{
  "abstract": "A rational operation on Diophantine tuple entries need not preserve their individual pair roots. We study the generic seven-entry field obtained by adjoining all 21 square roots of $1+a_i a_j$ and classify every dominant rational self-map of a smooth projective model. Every such map has degree one and is generated by entry permutations, simultaneous entry negation and independent root signs; the group is $(C_2)^{22}\\rtimes S_7$. The central invariant is the space of regular four-forms. We prove that its dimension is 56 and that there are no regular forms in degrees one through three. In Cayley coordinates, quadratic characters correspond to Eulerian graphs on eight vertices. A cyclic-cover calculation and multiplier ideals reduce completeness of the 56 explicit forms to cohomology of coordinate-plane unions. An elementary graph lemma and a monomial intersection-complex argument finish the calculation. The decomposable four-forms recover a Johnson graph, then 28 rational ratio fields. These force a common power map on the base, and its branch divisors force exponent plus or minus one. Two independent exhaustive implementations check every nontrivial character. As a construction constraint, rooted families on parameter spaces without regular four-forms have at most three algebraically independent entries, with the classical regular quadruple family showing sharpness. These results constrain universal operations and parameter spaces; they neither construct a rational septuple nor exclude special rational points, proper subvarieties or multivalued correspondences.",
